Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP) notice

This notice sits alongside the MIT License above and doesn't change it. It says what
the licence covers and what it doesn't.

1. The MIT License applies only to lect-kit's own code and documentation. It grants no
   rights in any community's language, knowledge, stories, songs, recordings,
   transcripts, names or other cultural expression.

2. Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property belongs to the communities it comes
   from. Those rights are held collectively and continue indefinitely. They don't
   depend on copyright, so material that is out of copyright or published long ago can
   still carry them.

3. Using lect-kit with a community's material gives no one, including the authors of
   lect-kit, any rights in that material. Anyone who uses the kit with a community's
   material is responsible for having that community's free, prior and informed
   consent, and for following its protocols, labels and terms of use.

4. No community's material should be used to train AI models without that
   community's consent. lect-kit doesn't need AI to work, and by default sends nothing
   to an AI service.

5. Labels, notices and terms that a community attaches to its material, such as
   Traditional Knowledge Labels, travel with that material through anything built with
   lect-kit. Don't remove or alter them without the community's agreement.

6. The Japanese worked example in examples/demo/ is drawn from public-domain sources
   and excludes material belonging to any Indigenous people.

7. This notice states the authors' position and the expectations of those who use the
   kit. It isn't legal advice, and Australian law doesn't fully protect ICIP. Where a
   community has its own protocols, those come first.

See PRINCIPLES.md (sections 5 and 13), docs/icip.md and docs/ai.md.
